## 5. Conventions from prior work the study should follow

### 5.1 Nested selection of k
Choosing k on the same data used to score it biases accuracy upward (Varma & Simon 2006). The
objective's "inner cross-validation" means a grid search over k inside each outer training fold
(e.g. `GridSearchCV` nested in the outer loop). The grid, the inner fold count and the tie rule
are not given by the objective: the analysis owner chooses and states them (a reasonable default:
odd k in 1..~31, stratified 5-fold inner CV, lowest k on ties), and reports the distribution of
chosen k over the 50 outer folds.

### 5.2 Intervals for repeated-CV estimates
The objective asks for "a 95% interval" without naming the method. The 50 fold scores are not
independent (overlapping training sets, repeated use of the same 150 points), so a naive
t-interval over 50 folds is too narrow and its tests have inflated type-I error (Dietterich 1998;
Nadeau & Bengio 2003; Bouckaert & Frank 2004). The established fix for repeated k-fold CV is the
corrected resampled t interval (Nadeau & Bengio 2003; applied to r x k-fold by Bouckaert & Frank
2004): variance multiplied by (1/n + n_test/n_train) = (1/50 + 1/4) with n = 50, t with 49 df.
Recommendation: use it for both the per-method means and the paired difference, and say so in
the table caption. Alternatives (naive t, percentile bootstrap over repetitions) may be shown
only if labelled. Note also that CV estimates average prediction error over training sets rather
than the error of the one fitted model (Bates, Hastie & Tibshirani 2024), which is the right
framing for "which method is more accurate on this data".

### 5.3 Preprocessing and settings to state
- k-NN is distance-based: standardising features (fit on the training fold only, in a pipeline)
  is the usual convention; whether to scale is the analysis owner's choice, but it must be stated
  and applied identically in inner and outer loops. Same for LR.
- LR: state solver, penalty and C (scikit-learn default is L2 with C=1.0, lbfgs, multinomial).
- Fix all seeds (outer split seeds per repetition, inner CV seed), record library versions, so
  the Ibex/local comparison (R10) is meaningful; identical numbers are expected with identical
  versions, small differences possible with different BLAS/library versions — report which.

### 5.4 Expected scale of results (planning expectation, not a measurement or a citation)
Both methods are expected to score in the mid-to-high 90s percent on Iris, with a difference of
about a percentage point or less; an interval for the difference that includes zero is a likely
and legitimate outcome and should be reported as such, not tuned away.

### 5.5 Figure and table (what they must show)
- The single table: per method, mean accuracy and 95% interval; the paired difference and its
  interval; the interval method; plus (compactly) the Ibex node name and identical-or-not result,
  unless those go in the text.
- The single figure: per-fold (or per-repetition) accuracies of both methods and/or the paired
  differences, with means and intervals marked, so the reader can see the spread the interval
  summarises. Optional inside the same figure: distribution of chosen k.

